<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Panj River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pyanj</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Panj is the upper course of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, formed by the junction of the Pamir and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wakhan">Wakhan</a> rivers. For much of its length of about 920 kilometres it forms the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and Tajikistan, a line fixed by Russian and British agreements of the 1890s. It joins the Vakhsh to become the Amu Darya.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/panj-river.jpg" alt="Panj River" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Background layer attributed to DEMIS Mapserver, map created by MTWT2012 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Panj_river.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
